UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 1: Inference from clues', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Infer unstated meaning by combining textual clues with careful reasoning.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Keep the independent response and two written check responses; record oral retelling or performance where required by the task.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Create a new example using a completely different character or setting, show the same skill, then explain to another person how you checked its accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"WE DO — attempt each prompt before comparing to the feedback.\n1. Infer why \"Ari reached for a jumper when the wind grew sharp\".\nCOMPARE: Ari likely felt cold or expected to feel cold. Clues: sharp wind and reaching for a jumper.\n2. What might \"Suri checked the road twice and kept holding her envelope\" suggest?\nCOMPARE: She may be anxious about delivering something or waiting for someone. Both behaviours suggest concern, but the text is not specific.\n3. Complete: \"I think the shop is closing because the sign says CLOSED and the worker turns the lights off.\"\nCOMPARE: Conclusion: shop is closing or closed. Evidence: sign and lights, not a guess about the worker''s mood.\n4. A child smiles at a note. Name two possible feelings and what extra clue would help decide.\nCOMPARE: Pride or amusement are plausible; reading the note or seeing the child''s next action would help.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"YOU DO — complete the following substantial original task, not a copied model.\nRead: \"The hall was silent. Theo clutched a folded paper, glanced at the stage and took a long breath. When his name was called, he stood up.\" Write three inferences about Theo or the situation, quote a specific clue for each, explain which inference is strongest and suggest one new detail that could change your interpretation.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Infer unstated meaning by combining textual clues with careful reasoning.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typing area","Pencil for optional evidence"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper to copy only the original independent prompt; have the child respond in writing or orally as required. Save a photograph or brief parent observation for the learning record.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance: Accept cautious supported inferences (nervous, excited, preparing to speak or receive an award), but do not treat an unstated event as confirmed. Require a quotation or exact clue each time. Accept plausible original solutions where the text supports them; require the learner to explain at least one independent choice.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Text: \"Nia wiped her muddy shoes before entering.\" Fact: shoes were muddy and she wiped them. Inference: she wanted to avoid making the floor dirty. The text does not directly state that reason.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Avoid quoting a clue that does not actually support the conclusion. Correction: Conclusion: shop is closing or closed. Evidence: sign and lights, not a guess about the worker''s mood.\n\nParent: Assess comprehension and language rather than penalising speech or motor difficulties unrelated to the objective. Keep written models out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A statement gives a fact directly; an inference is a reasonable conclusion based on clues. State both, so a reader can see your reasoning. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A statement gives a fact directly; an inference is a reasonable conclusion based on clues. State both, so a reader can see your reasoning.","Do not call an inferred feeling a stated fact when the text never names it.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Separate what the text says from what you infer\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Good inferences become stronger when several clues point towards the same idea; one clue may have different explanations. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Good inferences become stronger when several clues point towards the same idea; one clue may have different explanations.","Do not turn one vague clue into a certain explanation.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Gather more than one useful clue\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A strong answer follows: I infer __ because the text says __ and I know __. That final step is reasoning, not an invented quote. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A strong answer follows: I infer __ because the text says __ and I know __. That final step is reasoning, not an invented quote.","Avoid quoting a clue that does not actually support the conclusion.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Explain the bridge between evidence and conclusion\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Two explanations can fit one clue. Look for confirming details and admit what you cannot tell yet. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Two explanations can fit one clue. Look for confirming details and admit what you cannot tell yet.","A confident answer is not automatically accurate when details are missing.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Revise or limit an inference when evidence is weak\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Ari likely felt cold or expected to feel cold. Clues: sharp wind and reaching for a jumper. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["Ari likely felt cold or expected to feel cold. Clues: sharp wind and reaching for a jumper.","Do not call an inferred feeling a stated fact when the text never names it.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Infer why \"Ari reached for a jumper when the wind grew sharp\".\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"She may be anxious about delivering something or waiting for someone. Both behaviours suggest concern, but the text is not specific. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["She may be anxious about delivering something or waiting for someone. Both behaviours suggest concern, but the text is not specific.","Do not turn one vague clue into a certain explanation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"What might \"Suri checked the road twice and kept holding her envelope\" suggest?\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Conclusion: shop is closing or closed. Evidence: sign and lights, not a guess about the worker''s mood. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["Conclusion: shop is closing or closed. Evidence: sign and lights, not a guess about the worker''s mood.","Avoid quoting a clue that does not actually support the conclusion.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Complete: \"I think the shop is closing because the sign says CLOSED and the work\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Pride or amusement are plausible; reading the note or seeing the child''s next action would help. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["Pride or amusement are plausible; reading the note or seeing the child''s next action would help.","A confident answer is not automatically accurate when details are missing.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"A child smiles at a note. Name two possible feelings and what extra clue would h\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Meaningful understanding is shown when a learner transfers the method to new text or speech and explains their evidence.","marking_guide":"Require an original relevant example, accurate terminology and an explanation grounded in the example.","question":"Use your own example to demonstrate fact and explain the evidence supporting your choice.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Text: \"Nia wiped her muddy shoes before entering.\" Fact: shoes were muddy and she wiped them. Inference: she wanted to avoid making the floor dirty. The text does not directly state that reason.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"Explaining a correction helps the learner distinguish an effective strategy from a plausible-sounding but inaccurate approach.","marking_guide":"Require a genuine relevant error, accurate correction and a reason that refers to the target concept.","question":"Identify and correct a common mistake relating to evidence in this lesson.","sample_answer":"Common error: Avoid quoting a clue that does not actually support the conclusion. Correction: Conclusion: shop is closing or closed. Evidence: sign and lights, not a guess about the worker''s mood.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Submit your original independent work and your brief explanation of the key choices you made.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w04-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; compare the existing Coming Soon card and official syllabus content points before marking verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise fact.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Your goal is: Infer unstated meaning by combining textual clues with careful reasoning."],"guided":["Attempt each guided prompt without looking ahead.","Compare your attempt with the supplied specific feedback.","Guided comparison: She may be anxious about delivering something or waiting for someone. Both behaviours suggest concern, but the text is not specific."],"learn":["Study the first worked demonstration carefully.","Name the exact words or events that make it work.","Demonstration: Text: \"Nia wiped her muddy shoes before entering.\" Fact: shoes were muddy and she wiped them. Inference: she wanted to avoid making the floo"],"quiz":["Read the whole question and eliminate misconceptions.","Prefer the option that quotes or uses actual evidence.","Likely trap: Avoid quoting a clue that does not actually support the conclusion."],"submit":["Include your own response and a short reason.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation before sending.","Success looks like: Infer unstated meaning by combining textual clues with careful reasoning."],"teach0":["Focus on separate what the text says from what you infer.","Hold this teaching point: A statement gives a fact directly; an inference is a reasonable conclusion based on clues. State both, so a reader can s","Worked comparison: Ari likely felt cold or expected to feel cold. Clues: sharp wind and reaching for a jumper."],"teach1":["Focus on gather more than one useful clue.","Hold this teaching point: Good inferences become stronger when several clues point towards the same idea; one clue may have different explanations","Worked comparison: She may be anxious about delivering something or waiting for someone. Both behaviours suggest concern, but the text is n"],"teach2":["Focus on explain the bridge between evidence and conclusion.","Hold this teaching point: A strong answer follows: I infer __ because the text says __ and I know __. That final step is reasoning, not an invente","Worked comparison: Conclusion: shop is closing or closed. Evidence: sign and lights, not a guess about the worker''s mood."],"teach3":["Focus on revise or limit an inference when evidence is weak.","Hold this teaching point: Two explanations can fit one clue. Look for confirming details and admit what you cannot tell yet.","Worked comparison: Pride or amusement are plausible; reading the note or seeing the child''s next action would help."],"write":["Use the independent instructions, not the model words.","Give an original response and the reason behind it.","Independent reminder: Read: \"The hall was silent. Theo clutched a folded paper, glanced at the stage and took a long breath. When his name was called, he stood up.\" Write three infer"]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use fact with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify inference and point to the words or events that support it.","I can practise evidence independently on a new prompt.","I can notice interpretation needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying fact independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to separate what the text says from what you infer?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nText: \"Nia wiped her muddy shoes before entering.\" Fact: shoes were muddy and she wiped them. Inference: she wanted to avoid making the floor dirty. The text does not directly state that reason.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nInfer why \"Ari reached for a jumper when the wind grew sharp\".\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nAri likely felt cold or expected to feel cold. Clues: sharp wind and reaching for a jumper.","explain":"A statement gives a fact directly; an inference is a reasonable conclusion based on clues. State both, so a reader can see your reasoning.\n\nA statement gives a fact directly; an inference is a reasonable conclusion based on clues. State both, so a reader can see your reasoning. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not call an inferred feeling a stated fact when the text never names it. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Separate what the text says from what you infer","visual":[{"alt":"Stated fact of wiping muddy shoes beside the inference that Nia wanted a clean floor.","caption":"An inference is a reasoned conclusion; the text did not name the reason.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stated fact</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Nia wiped her muddy shoes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Inference</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">She did not want a dirty floor</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.","who":"juno"},{"text":"So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Text: \"Nia wiped her muddy shoes before entering.\" Fact: shoes were muddy and she wiped them. Inference: she wanted to a Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.\nPip: So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Text: \"Nia wiped her muddy shoes before entering.\" Fact: shoes were muddy and she wiped them. Inference: she wanted to a Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying inference independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to gather more than one useful clue?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"The empty lunchbox rattled. Jax looked at the clock and sighed.\" I infer Jax is hungry or disappointed. An empty box, clock-check and sigh together support the idea.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nWhat might \"Suri checked the road twice and kept holding her envelope\" suggest?\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nShe may be anxious about delivering something or waiting for someone. Both behaviours suggest concern, but the text is not specific.","explain":"Good inferences become stronger when several clues point towards the same idea; one clue may have different explanations.\n\nGood inferences become stronger when several clues point towards the same idea; one clue may have different explanations. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not turn one vague clue into a certain explanation. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Gather more than one useful clue","visual":[{"alt":"Empty lunchbox, clock glance and sigh supporting the idea that Jax may be hungry.","caption":"Several clues pointing the same way make an inference stronger.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Several clues</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">empty lunchbox, clock, sigh</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stronger idea</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Jax may be hungry</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying evidence independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to explain the bridge between evidence and conclusion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"The grass glittered white at dawn.\" I infer frost is possible because white glitter on early morning grass often comes from ice crystals in cold weather. But it could be another source; I need more context.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nComplete: \"I think the shop is closing because the sign says CLOSED and the worker turns the lights off.\"\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nConclusion: shop is closing or closed. Evidence: sign and lights, not a guess about the worker''s mood.","explain":"A strong answer follows: I infer __ because the text says __ and I know __. That final step is reasoning, not an invented quote.\n\nA strong answer follows: I infer __ because the text says __ and I know __. That final step is reasoning, not an invented quote. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nAvoid quoting a clue that does not actually support the conclusion. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Explain the bridge between evidence and conclusion"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying interpretation independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to revise or limit an inference when evidence is weak?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"The puppy hid under a chair.\" It may be frightened, tired or playing. If the next line mentions thunder, fear becomes a stronger inference.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nA child smiles at a note. Name two possible feelings and what extra clue would help decide.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nPride or amusement are plausible; reading the note or seeing the child''s next action would help.","explain":"Two explanations can fit one clue. Look for confirming details and admit what you cannot tell yet.\n\nTwo explanations can fit one clue. Look for confirming details and admit what you cannot tell yet. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nA confident answer is not automatically accurate when details are missing. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Revise or limit an inference when evidence is weak"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 1: Inference from clues","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: story elements and planning","week":4,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"I DO — Text: \"Nia wiped her muddy shoes before entering.\" Fact: shoes were muddy and she wiped them. Inference: she wanted to avoid making the floor dirty. The text does not directly state that reason.\n\nNow look for the exact evidence in the words or actions. \"The empty lunchbox rattled. Jax looked at the clock and sighed.\" I infer Jax is hungry or disappointed. An empty box, clock-check and sigh together support the idea.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Stated fact of wiping muddy shoes beside the inference that Nia wanted a clean floor.","caption":"An inference is a reasoned conclusion; the text did not name the reason.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\